              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "js-yaml: js-yaml: Denial of Service via crafted YAML merge keys"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "js-yaml is a JavaScript YAML parser and dumper. Prior to 4.2.0 and 3.15.0, a crafted YAML document can trigger algorithmic CPU exhaustion in js-yaml merge-key processing (\u003c\u003c) by repeating the same alias many times in a merge sequence. This causes quadratic parse-time behavior relative to input size and can block a Node.js worker/event loop for seconds with a relatively small payload (tens of KB), resulting in denial of service. The issue is in merge handling inside lib/loader.js. This vulnerability is fixed in 4.2.0 and 3.15.0."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53550",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-53550\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: js-yaml\nFixed Version: 4.2.0, 3.15.0\nLink: [CVE-2026-53550](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53550)\njs-yaml is a JavaScript YAML parser and dumper. Prior to 4.2.0 and 3.15.0, a crafted YAML document can trigger algorithmic CPU exhaustion in js-yaml merge-key processing (\u003c\u003c) by repeating the same alias many times in a merge sequence. This causes quadratic parse-time behavior relative to input size and can block a Node.js worker/event loop for seconds with a relatively small payload (tens of KB), resulting in denial of service. The issue is in merge handling inside lib/loader.js. This vulnerability is fixed in 4.2.0 and 3.15.0.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-53550**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|js-yaml|4.2.0, 3.15.0|[CVE-2026-53550](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-53550)|\n\njs-yaml is a JavaScript YAML parser and dumper. Prior to 4.2.0 and 3.15.0, a crafted YAML document can trigger algorithmic CPU exhaustion in js-yaml merge-key processing (\u003c\u003c) by repeating the same alias many times in a merge sequence. This causes quadratic parse-time behavior relative to input size and can block a Node.js worker/event loop for seconds with a relatively small payload (tens of KB), resulting in denial of service. The issue is in merge handling inside lib/loader.js. This vulnerability is fixed in 4.2.0 and 3.15.0."
              },
