            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-33896",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "node-forge: Forge (node-forge): Certificate validation bypass allows unauthorized certificate issuance"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Forge (also called `node-forge`) is a native implementation of Transport Layer Security in JavaScript. Prior to version 1.4.0, `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` does not enforce RFC 5280 basicConstraints requirements when an intermediate certificate lacks both the `basicConstraints` and `keyUsage` extensions. This allows any leaf certificate (without these extensions) to act as a CA and sign other certificates, which node-forge will accept as valid. Version 1.4.0 patches the issue."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-33896",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-33896\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: node-forge\nFixed Version: 1.4.0\nLink: [CVE-2026-33896](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-33896)\nForge (also called `node-forge`) is a native implementation of Transport Layer Security in JavaScript. Prior to version 1.4.0, `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` does not enforce RFC 5280 basicConstraints requirements when an intermediate certificate lacks both the `basicConstraints` and `keyUsage` extensions. This allows any leaf certificate (without these extensions) to act as a CA and sign other certificates, which node-forge will accept as valid. Version 1.4.0 patches the issue.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-33896**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|node-forge|1.4.0|[CVE-2026-33896](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-33896)|\n\nForge (also called `node-forge`) is a native implementation of Transport Layer Security in JavaScript. Prior to version 1.4.0, `pki.verifyCertificateChain()` does not enforce RFC 5280 basicConstraints requirements when an intermediate certificate lacks both the `basicConstraints` and `keyUsage` extensions. This allows any leaf certificate (without these extensions) to act as a CA and sign other certificates, which node-forge will accept as valid. Version 1.4.0 patches the issue."
